{"t":1793111454,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"86186dc9-aaaf-4603-a827-20f338332e41","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-27T14:13:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 271413\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n1013 AM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo changes to the forecast.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Benign weather continues through the remainder of October\n  with near to above normal temperatures expected. \n \n- 2) Precipitation chances return in early November.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Benign weather continues through the remainder \nof October with near to above normal temperatures expected. \n\nA weak cold front will stall to the south and east this\nafternoon. By Wednesday morning, the same front will retreat \nback to the north as a warm front. Another large area of high \npressure will build to the north Thursday through Saturday. As a\nresult, expect passing high clouds and a series of wind shifts \nwith each of the frontal passages. Winds will remain light out \nof the north and northwest this afternoon. Winds will change to\nthe south and southwest late Wednesday through Friday. This \nwill yield seasonable to above normal temperatures for the \nremainder of the workweek. Highs will generally range from the \nmid 60s and low 70s though Wednesday with low to mid 70s \nThursday and mid to upper 70s Friday. This is thanks to the \nposition of the high to the north leading to increased southeast\nto southwest winds across the region. Overnight lows will \nremain mild as well in the mid 40s and low 50s.\n\nThere is the potential for some low clouds, fog, or even a\nlittle drizzle during the late night into morning hours each of\nthe next few days as onshore flow develops.\n\nOtherwise, dry conditions will continue through Saturday, \nalthough this will be a day of transition as the pattern begins \nto loosen. Upper-level ridging will buckle with an incoming \ntrough from the Midwest region. This trough will ultimately \nbring an end to the dry conditions with increasing cloud cover \nand precipitation chances heading into the first few days of \nNovember. Despite the increased cloud cover, expect highs \nSaturday back in the upper 60s and low 70s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Precipitation chances return in early November.\n\nUpper-level ridging overhead breaks down into zonal flow by \nSunday. Conditions likely start to become cloudier by the second\nhalf of the weekend as troughing develops over the Ohio River \nValley before heading east Sunday into Monday. This will bring\nthe first chances for precipitation over the Mid-Atlantic in \nover a week, although overall breadth and total rainfall from \nthis system remain uncertain at this time.\n\nTemperatures likely drop back to the low 70s over the weekend \nin the wake of another weak (dry) front. With overnight lows \ngenerally remaining in the 40s and 50s, this ranges somewhat \nabove normal for the beginning of November. Looking ahead, model\nguidance hints at a second, potentially more potent upper-level\ntrough approaching the area that could bring additional chances\nfor precipitation by the middle of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /14Z TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nPrevailing VFR conditions are expected through tonight. Winds \nwill remain light out of the north to northeast. Light onshore\nflow could lead to some patchy low clouds/fog in outlying areas\nlate tonight.\n\nWinds will shift to the southeast Wednesday ahead of a warm \nfrontal boundary. This will lead to the potential for low \nclouds and fog, especially at CHO and MRB given the marine \ninfluence/increased surface moisture banking along the eastern \nside of the Blue Ridge mountains and up the Potomac River Valley.\nConfidence in this is low, although hi-res models have shown \nsome consistency in recent cycles. Aside from early morning low\nclouds and/or fog, VFR conditions are expected Wednesday night \nthrough Saturday as high pressure builds along the coast. Winds \nwill turn south to southwesterly Thursday before becoming \neasterly Saturday. The next chance for rain showers occurs \nSunday with an incoming cold front, but uncertainty remains high\nat this time.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nLight northeast to east winds expected through tonight.\n\nWinds become southeasterly Wednesday before turning southerly \nThursday. Some channeling is possible at times, though overall \ngradients look weak. Winds stay below SCA thresholds through the\nend of the month. Southerly winds shift westerly on Friday, \nthen northeasterly on Saturday.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nLight onshore flow Wednesday into Thursday may push sensitive \nshoreline close to minor flood stage. Areas most at risk for\nreaching minor tidal flood thresholds would be places like \nAnnapolis and Alexandria mainly during the evening tide cycles \n(which are astronomically higher).\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...DHOF/EST/SRT\nAVIATION...DHOF/EST/SRT\nMARINE...DHOF/EST/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793111400,"days":{"2026-10-27":{"hi":70,"lo":48,"pop":2,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-28":{"hi":68,"lo":49,"pop":2,"short":"Patchy Fog then Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-29":{"hi":73,"lo":51,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-30":{"hi":77,"lo":51,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-31":{"hi":70,"lo":51,"pop":4,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-01":{"hi":71,"lo":57,"pop":50,"short":"Mostly Cloudy then Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-11-02":{"hi":71,"lo":56,"pop":50,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793109600,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,22500,21700,null,19800,19800,19400,19100,25000,25000,25000,null,nul